Using an `ArgumentsAccessor` as the parameter...
```java
	@ParameterizedTest
	@MethodSource("arrayStream2") void test2(ArgumentsAccessor accessor) {
		for (var object : accessor.toList()) {
			System.out.print(object.getClass() + " -> " + object);
			if (object instanceof int[]) {
				System.out.println(" = " + Arrays.toString((int[]) object));
			}
			else {
				System.out.println(" ?!");
			}
		}
	}
```
....yields:
```
class [I -> [I@8e0379d = [1, 2]
class [I -> [I@161b062a = [2, 3]
```

We currently do not support multi-dimensional arrays within a `Stream` returned by a factory method used with `@MethodSource`.

The workaround is to generate a stream of arguments (i.e., `Stream<Arguments>`) as follows.

```java
static Stream<Arguments> arrayStream2() {
	return Stream.of(arguments((Object) new Object[][] { { 1, 2 } }),
		arguments((Object) new Object[][] { { 2, 3 } }));
}

@ParameterizedTest
@MethodSource("arrayStream2")
void test2(Object[][] par) {
	System.err.println(Arrays.deepToString(par));
}
```

The following is another workaround that is similar to the above `Arguments` approach.

```java
static Stream<Object[]> arrayStream3() {
	return Stream.of(new Object[] { new int[][] { { 1, 2 } } }, new Object[] { new int[][] { { 2, 3 } } });
}

@ParameterizedTest
@MethodSource("arrayStream3")
void test3(int[][] par) {
	System.err.println(Arrays.deepToString(par));
}
```

And the following is a revised version of the `Arguments` approach that retains the desired `int[][]` parameter type.

```java
static Stream<Arguments> arrayStream4() {
	return Stream.of(arguments((Object) new int[][] { { 1, 2 } }), arguments((Object) new int[][] { { 2, 3 } }));
}

@ParameterizedTest
@MethodSource("arrayStream4")
void test4(int[][] par) {
	System.err.println(Arrays.deepToString(par));
}
```

FYI: I've pushed a _WIP_ to the following feature branch.

https://github.com/junit-team/junit5/commits/issues/1665-method-source-n-dimensional-arrays

@junit-team/junit-lambda, now up for _team discussion_.
